* ============================================================================
* cnx_realtime_index_proof.dts
*
* Proves XIDX-TXN-02 M1: a CNX order is maintained in REALTIME. A REPLACE that
* moves an indexed value re-places that record in the ordering immediately,
* with NO REBUILD in between, and the engine does NOT warn about staleness
* because there is nothing stale to warn about.
*
* This is the positive counterpart to index_maintenance_failure_proof.dts
* (regression IDXSTALE), which asserts that a backend that CANNOT maintain
* incrementally says so. Same fixture, same markers, opposite expectation --
* deliberately, so the pair reads as one contract with two halves:
*     maintenance happens        -> here (CNXLIVE)
*     maintenance cannot happen  -> IDXSTALE, and the engine is honest
* This file is the direct descendant of the IDXSTALE run that inverted when M1
* landed; keeping both is what makes the inversion legible instead of looking
* like a regression someone silently retuned.
*
* HOW CNX MAINTAINS A KEYLESS INDEX
*   A CNX RUN1 payload stores 4 bytes per recno and NO keys
*   (cnx_document.cpp:81 builds every entry as InxEntry{"", rn}). Order lives
*   in the SEQUENCE of entries, so CnxBackend::upsert cannot binary-search
*   stored keys -- there are none. It searches the PERMUTATION instead,
*   comparing the edited record's live field value against the live field value
*   of the record at each probe position (~log2 n record reads). The table is
*   the ordering authority, which is the same authority a REBUILD uses, and
*   both paths share derive_sort_entry_/sort_entry_less_ so they cannot drift.
*
* MARKER DESIGN -- READ BEFORE EDITING
*   Inherited wholesale from IDXSTALE, and load-bearing for the same reasons:
*     1. Park the cursor on a KNOWN DIFFERENT record before every SEEK, then
*        assert the FIELD VALUE moved. A SEEK that silently no-ops fails the
*        guard instead of inheriting a passing position.
*     2. Markers must be STRING comparisons. "? <str> + FOUND()" and
*        "? <str> + (RECNO() = 1)" both render EMPTY, which reads as failure.
*        ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "X" renders .T./.F. correctly. Wrapping RECNO() in
*        STR() does NOT rescue it -- the problem is RECNO() itself.
*   A version of this proof that skipped rule 1 passed while proving nothing.
*   Do not remove the GO guards.
*
* Self-contained: builds and erases its own throwaway v32 table; never touches
* STUDENTS or any live fixture.
*   Standalone:  DO cnx_realtime_index_proof
*   Regression:  REGRESSION RUN CNXLIVE
*
* Runtime status: NOT YET PROVEN AS A REGISTERED SCRIPT. The behaviour it
* asserts was runtime-proven 2026-07-31 on wsl-lean (TOP moved from rec 2 to
* rec 1 with no REBUILD, leftStale=no, warning absent); this file packages that
* measurement as a repeatable test.
* ============================================================================
SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

* x32 lane: CNX is the v32 indexing backend
DO x32

FORMULA "CNX-REALTIME-INDEX-PROOF-BEGIN"

* --- throwaway v32 table (bare CREATE defaults to MSDOS/DBASE = v32) --------
CREATE CNXLIVE (SID N(8), LNAME C(20))

APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 1
REPLACE LNAME WITH "MILLER"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 2
REPLACE LNAME WITH "ANDERSON"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 3
REPLACE LNAME WITH "ZEBRA"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 4
REPLACE LNAME WITH "YOUNG"

* --- CNX index on LNAME ------------------------------------------------------
CNX CREATE
CNX ADDTAG LNAME
REBUILD
SET ORDER TAG LNAME

* --- G0: the ORDER IS LIVE ---------------------------------------------------
* Guards everything below. Park on 4 (YOUNG), seek a key that lives on 1.
GO 4
SEEK "MILLER"
? "L_G0_seek_works_before_mutation:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "MILLER")

* --- G1: baseline ORDER, before any mutation ---------------------------------
* Under LNAME order the first row must be ANDERSON. L_T2 compares against this,
* so it is taken before anything moves. If G1 fails, T2 proves nothing.
GO TOP
? "L_G1_baseline_top_is_ANDERSON:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ANDERSON")

* --- the mutation under test -------------------------------------------------
* Expect EXACTLY ONE line:
*   REPLACE: Replaced field #2 at rec 1.
* The staleness warning must be ABSENT. Trace should read
* "staleBefore=no leftStale=no" -- no false->true transition, because the
* backend maintained the order rather than giving up on it.
GO 1
REPLACE LNAME WITH "AAAAA"

* --- L_T1: the RECORD WRITE LANDED -------------------------------------------
* Unchanged from IDXSTALE. Realtime maintenance must not cost the write.
GO 1
? "L_T1_record_carries_new_value:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

* --- L_T2: THE ORDER IS ALREADY CORRECT -- the whole point --------------------
* This is the marker that inverted when M1 landed, and it is the reason this
* file exists. AAAAA now sorts before ANDERSON, so an ordered walk must start
* at record 1 IMMEDIATELY -- no REBUILD, no REINDEX, nothing between the
* REPLACE above and the GO TOP below.
*
* Under the pre-M1 stub backend this read ANDERSON (see IDXSTALE E_T2). If it
* reads ANDERSON here, realtime CNX maintenance has regressed to batch.
GO TOP
? "L_T2_realtime_order_top_is_AAAAA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

* --- L_T3: the record that did NOT move is still correctly placed ------------
* Guards against "maintenance" that reorders by accident -- e.g. an insert that
* appends instead of placing, which would leave position 0 right while
* corrupting everything after it. ANDERSON must now be SECOND, directly after
* AAAAA. Reached by SKIP from TOP rather than by seek, so it reads the ORDER
* and not a lookup.
GO TOP
SKIP
? "L_T3_second_row_is_ANDERSON:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ANDERSON")

* --- L_T4: the tail of the order survived the edit ---------------------------
* A permutation that lost or duplicated an entry can still look right at the
* top. ZEBRA sorts last of the four and must still be there.
BOTTOM
? "L_T4_bottom_is_ZEBRA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

* --- L_T5: SEEK agrees with the maintained order -----------------------------
* Park on the bottom, seek the moved record. Confirms lookup and order tell the
* same story after realtime maintenance -- on a STALE index they can disagree,
* which is exactly what IDXSTALE E_T3 documents.
GO 3
SEEK "AAAAA"
? "L_T5_seek_reaches_moved_record:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

* --- L_T6: a REBUILD is a NO-OP now, not a repair ----------------------------
* IDXSTALE E_T4 proves REBUILD repairs a stale order. Here there is nothing to
* repair, so REBUILD must leave the order exactly as realtime maintenance
* already had it. Same assertion as L_T2, taken after a rebuild: if realtime
* and rebuild disagree, the two orderings have drifted and one of them is
* wrong.
REBUILD
SET ORDER TAG LNAME
GO TOP
? "L_T6_rebuild_agrees_with_realtime:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "AAAAA")

FORMULA "CNX-REALTIME-INDEX-PROOF-END"

* --- teardown ----------------------------------------------------------------
* No bare "SET ORDER TO" -- it reports "SET ORDER: missing target."
* CLOSE drops the order with the table.
CLOSE
ERASE CNXLIVE CONFIRM
